# Tutorial 15 — UI Layout and Theme
# How to customize dialogue boxes, menu screens, and visual themes for your game.

@scenario {{SCENARIO_PREFIX}}_tutorial_15_ui_layout
@include {{CHARACTERS_INCLUDE}}
{{BG_DECL}}@label start
{{BG_START}}[inc tutorial_count]
[textspeed 28]
[autodelay 1650]
[show lavender center happy]
[wait 200]
Lavender: Your game's UI is the frame around every moment. A good frame disappears — a bad one distracts.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Let's talk about how to customize the dialogue box, menus, and visual theme to fit your game's identity.

@label lesson_menu
[show lavender far_left talking]
Lavender: What would you like to learn about?
> The dialogue box -> section_dialogue_box
> Menu screens -> section_menus
> Styles and colors -> section_styles
> Layout files explained -> section_layout_files
> Theme consistency -> section_consistency
> Quick reference card -> reference_sheet
> Practice scene -> practice_scene
> Back to Tutorial Hub -> return_hub

# ─── The Dialogue Box ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_dialogue_box
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The dialogue box is what the player stares at the most. It needs to be readable, attractive, and out of the way.
narrator: {b}Key properties:{/b}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}textBoxX, textBoxY{/color} — position on screen (normalized 0–1).
narrator: {color=#8888cc}textBoxWidth, textBoxHeight{/color} — size of the box.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}textBoxOpacity{/color} — transparency of the background.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}textBoxAsset{/color} — custom image for the box background.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The layout file also controls the name box, dialogue padding, and choice button placement.
Lavender: You edit these in {b}config/ui/dialogue.layout{/b} — or use the visual editor to drag things around.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: A good default: semi-transparent dark box at the bottom third of the screen. Don't cover the characters.
> Next: Menu screens -> section_menus
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Menu Screens ────────────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_menus
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Menu screens — title menu, save/load, settings — each have their own layout and style files.
narrator: {b}Layout:{/b} Where items appear. List position, line height, text alignment.
narrator: {b}Style:{/b} How items look. Colors, fonts, selected/disabled states.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: The file structure:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}config/menu/screens/main.menu{/color} — the main menu definition.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}config/menu/layouts/main.layout{/color} — positioning for that menu.
narrator: {color=#8888cc}config/menu/styles/main.style{/color} — colors and fonts for that menu.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Each menu screen references a layout and a style. Change the style, and the menu looks different without moving anything. Change the layout, and things move without changing color.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: The separation means your artist and your designer can work independently.
> Next: Styles -> section_styles
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Styles and Colors ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_styles
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Style files control the visual identity of your UI. Here's what you can set:
narrator: {b}itemColor{/b} — default text color for menu items.
narrator: {b}itemSelectedColor{/b} — color when hovered or selected.
narrator: {b}itemDisabledColor{/b} — grayed-out items the player can't click.
narrator: {b}fontFamily{/b} — the typeface. Pick one that matches your game's tone.
narrator: {b}fontSize{/b} — size in pixels.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: For the dialogue box, the style file controls:
narrator: {b}textColor{/b} — dialogue text color.
narrator: {b}nameTextColor{/b} — the speaking character's name.
narrator: {b}choiceBgColor{/b} — background of choice buttons.
narrator: {b}choiceTextColor{/b} — text on choice buttons.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Stick to 2–3 colors maximum. Consistency is more important than complexity.
> Next: Layout files -> section_layout_files
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Layout Files Explained ──────────────────────────────────────

@label section_layout_files
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Layout files are key-value properties. Here's a simplified example for a menu:
narrator: {color=#8888cc}listYStart = 0.35{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}lineHeight = 50.0{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}listWidthFactor = 0.56{/color}
narrator: {color=#8888cc}textAlign = center{/color}
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: All values are either normalized (0–1 for position ratios) or pixel-based (for sizes like line height and font size).
Lavender: The visual editors let you tweak these interactively — change a value, see it update live.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: But understanding the raw file means you can copy-paste layouts between projects, version control them, and diff changes.
> Next: Theme consistency -> section_consistency
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Theme Consistency ───────────────────────────────────────────

@label section_consistency
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A consistent theme means the player never thinks about the UI — it just feels right.
narrator: {b}Use the same font family{/b} across dialogue, menus, and choices.
narrator: {b}Use the same color palette{/b} — derive selected, disabled, and hover colors from the same base hue.
narrator: {b}Match opacity and corner radius{/b} between the dialogue box and menu backgrounds.
narrator: {b}Test at your target resolution.{/b} UI that looks good at 1080p might be too small at 720p.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: When in doubt, keep it simple. A clean, minimal UI beats an overdesigned one every time.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Players remember your story, not your menu borders. Make the UI invisible.
> Reference card -> reference_sheet
> Try it yourself -> practice_scene
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Reference Card ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label reference_sheet
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: UI layout and theme cheat sheet:
narrator: {b}Dialogue layout:{/b} config/ui/dialogue.layout — text box position, size, padding.
narrator: {b}Dialogue style:{/b} config/ui/dialogue.style — colors, fonts, opacity.
narrator: {b}Menu layout:{/b} config/menu/layouts/*.layout — list position, line height, alignment.
narrator: {b}Menu style:{/b} config/menu/styles/*.style — item colors, fonts, button assets.
narrator: {b}Menu screen:{/b} config/menu/screens/*.menu — screen definition referencing layout + style.
narrator: {b}Registry:{/b} config/menu/registry/menu.registry — maps which menus exist and their default.
narrator: {b}Tip:{/b} Separate layout (where) from style (how it looks). Edit independently.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Use the visual editors for quick iteration. Use the raw files for precision and version control.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

# ─── Practice Scene ──────────────────────────────────────────────

@label practice_scene
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Let's think about UI design decisions. Pick a scenario:
> Designing a horror game UI -> practice_horror
> Making a cheerful VN -> practice_cheerful
> A minimalist approach -> practice_minimal

@label practice_horror
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Horror game. Dark, moody. What does the UI look like?
Lavender: Low opacity text box — almost transparent. Dark red accents. A serif font, maybe slightly distressed.
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: The dialogue box should feel like it's barely there. The game world is what matters. The UI lurks.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Choice buttons: dark background, faint border, subtle glow on hover. Make the player feel uneasy even in the menu.
[set practice_result 1]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_cheerful
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: A cheerful, cozy VN. Bright colors, rounded corners, playful font.
Lavender: The text box should be warm — a soft cream or pastel background. High opacity for readability.
[show lavender right talking]
Lavender: Choice buttons: colorful, bouncy hover animation, friendly text. The UI should make the player smile before they even read a word.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: Match the dialogue box style to the menu style. Same fonts, same palette. Consistency is warmth.
[set practice_result 2]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_minimal
[show lavender left talking]
Lavender: Minimalist. Clean lines, lots of white space, a single accent color.
Lavender: The text box: sharp edges, thin border, no background image — just a semi-transparent solid color.
[show lavender center talking]
Lavender: Menus: centered text, generous line height, no icons. Let the typography do the heavy lifting.
[show lavender center happy]
Lavender: This approach scales beautifully to any resolution and works with any content. Less is genuinely more.
[set practice_result 3]
[jump practice_wrap]

@label practice_wrap
[show lavender center happy]
[if practice_result == 1]
Lavender: Horror UI is about absence — what you don't show. Let the darkness do the design work.
[elif practice_result == 2]
Lavender: Cheerful UI is about warmth — soft colors, readable fonts, and consistency that feels like a hug.
[else]
Lavender: Minimalism is the hardest to get wrong. When in doubt, remove elements until only the essential remains.
[endif]
Lavender: Whatever your style, test it with real content. A pretty mockup means nothing if the text is unreadable during gameplay.
> Back to menu -> lesson_menu

@label return_hub
[goto {{HUB_TARGET}}:topics_page_3]
